@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
title Claude in Chrome from a sandbox — the native host socket, carried across the boundary

' covers: internal/chromebridge/** (ScopeGate), defs/claudecode/mcp/proveo-lib/chrome-bridge.js, packages/lib/entrypoint-lib.sh (proveo_chrome_bridge, _proveo_chrome_scope_ok), internal/run/choices.go, internal/egress/plan.go (HostBridge)
'
' ── WHY A RELAY EXISTS AT ALL ──────────────────────────────────────────────
' Claude Code's browser integration is two processes on one machine: the
' claude-in-chrome MCP server inside the CLI, and a native messaging host that
' Chrome spawns for the extension. They meet on a Unix socket the NATIVE HOST
' listens on — /tmp/claude-mcp-browser-bridge-<username>/<pid>.sock — which the
' CLI connects to. Put the CLI in a container and nothing listens there: the
' native host is on the operator's machine with Chrome. Anthropic's position is
' that the integration does not cross that boundary
' (anthropics/claude-code#25506, #21299).
'
' The bridge carries the socket across instead. The host half is a TCP listener
' that, per connection, dials the newest native host socket and pipes bytes both
' ways; the container half listens where Claude Code looks and dials the relay.
' Claude Code on either end is unmodified.
'
' TCP RATHER THAN A BIND-MOUNTED SOCKET because virtiofs (Docker Desktop,
' OrbStack) does not carry Unix sockets, and every proveo host that matters is a
' Mac. The listener is therefore guarded by a per-run token the container relay
' sends as its first line; a connection that does not present it is closed
' before a byte reaches the browser.
'
' BindAddr: Docker Desktop and OrbStack route host.docker.internal to the host's
' loopback, so 127.0.0.1 is enough there and exposes nothing to the LAN. Linux's
' host-gateway is the bridge address, which loopback does not answer on — and
' there the token is what stands between the LAN and the operator's browser.
'
' Available is a MOMENT-IN-TIME answer, which is the honest one for a picker:
' the native host lives only while Chrome runs with the extension connected.
'
' ── THE CREDENTIAL GATE, MIRRORED FROM CLAUDE CODE 2.1.258 ─────────────────
' Knobs: PROVEO_CHROME_BRIDGE=host:port + PROVEO_CHROME_BRIDGE_TOKEN, both set
' by `proveo run` when the "claude-in-chrome" add-on is on.
'
' ── ENABLED ON sbx 2026-09-03. THE OLD PREMISE WAS FALSE ───────────────────
' Every "a sandbox VM cannot name the host" claim below was FALSE for sbx 0.39.0
' on darwin, and the exclusion that rested on it is gone.
' Measured from inside a throwaway `sbx create shell` sandbox against a host
' listener on 0.0.0.0:47713:
'
'   curl http://host.docker.internal:47713/            -> HOST-REACHED
'   curl --noproxy '*' http://host.docker.internal/…   -> HOST-REACHED
'   raw socket to host.docker.internal:47713           -> connected
'   raw socket to the host's LAN IP 192.168.50.195     -> connected
'
' Inside, host.docker.internal resolves to fe80::1 and answers. The
' --noproxy and raw-socket cases matter: they rule out proxy mediation, so a
' plain TCP bridge — which is what internal/chromebridge is — has a path.
'
' LOOPBACK IS ENOUGH — the one result that corrected the plan. A first probe
' used a 0.0.0.0 listener, which suggested the sbx path would need the wider
' bind the Linux docker branch takes. Retested against a 127.0.0.1-ONLY
' listener: host.docker.internal from inside the sandbox reaches the host's
' LOOPBACK and answers. So BindAddr needs no change and nothing is exposed to
' the LAN on either backend.
'
' ── WHAT ENABLING IT ACTUALLY TOOK ─────────────────────────────────────────
'   the host half   ""startChromeBridge"" is now shared by both backends, with the
'                   tier guard passed IN rather than hardcoded: that constraint
'                   is the docker backend's, where a non-open tier parks the
'                   agent behind a sidecar. sbx has no proveo sidecar and the
'                   tier is inert there, so it passes "".
'   the Kit env     ""sandbox.Input.BridgeEnv"" carries PROVEO_CHROME_BRIDGE and
'                   the token into the Kit's env, which is the only way they
'                   reach a sandbox.
'   the container   ""proveo_seed"" starts the relay. sbx never runs the image
'                   entrypoint, so the seed — its startup command — is the only
'                   place it can come up. The entrypoint's own call was REMOVED:
'                   both backends run the seed, so two callers meant two relays
'                   on docker and Claude Code choosing a socket by mtime. A
'                   PROVEO_CHROME_READY guard is the belt.
'   the picker      the ""sandboxTicked"" exclusion is gone.
'
' THE SURVIVAL QUESTION, MEASURED. proveo_seed runs as the Kit's startup command
' and EXITS, so a backgrounded relay has to outlive it. It does: setsid detaches
' it, the process is reparented, and a SEPARATE later exec finds the socket
' still listening. e2e/chrome_bridge_sbx_test.go asserts exactly that shape —
' start in one exec, connect from another — plus the five hops end to end and
' the token refusing an unauthenticated connection.
'
' Both chains pass: the docker twin in e2e/chrome_bridge_test.go and the sbx one
' beside it. Note the image bakes entrypoint-lib.sh, so a seed change is inert
' until `proveo build claudecode`.
'
' ── WHY THE PICKER BOX AND THE SANDBOX BOX ARE EXCLUSIVE ───────────────────
' HostBridge is what makes host.docker.internal resolve to the REAL host
' gateway. OFF, the open+forward path pins that name to the container's own
' loopback on purpose: an agent that can name the host can probe it. Only the
' open+forward paths honour it, and the picker greys the add-on elsewhere.
'
' ── THE SCOPE GATE EXISTS TWICE, AND A TEST RUNS ONE TABLE THROUGH BOTH ────
' chromebridge.ScopeGate on the host, so the picker can grey the box, and
' _proveo_chrome_scope_ok in entrypoint-lib.sh, so the container can warn when
' the box was ticked anyway. Two copies of one rule drift, and the drift is
' INVISIBLE: each side is silent in exactly the case the other one talks about.
' So the table is run through both and they must agree, shape for shape. That
' test also pins bash 3.2 — /bin/bash on macOS is the oldest shell an image
' entrypoint has to survive, and `case " $scopes "` is the pattern that works.
'
' The synthesis, spelled out:
'   CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN -> CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_SCOPES, else "user:inference"
'   ...FILE_DESCRIPTOR      -> a default carrying user:ccr_inference
'   a persisted /login      -> its real scopes, which include user:profile
' ANTHROPIC_API_KEY does not shadow a login, and is only fatal on its own.
'
' A BLANKED credential file is invisible to the gate, and that is its own case:
' it reasons about the session's SHAPE, and a husk classifies as nothing at
' all — so the gate fell silent and offered a bridge the container would
' refuse. The host can fall back to the Keychain; the container cannot, so in
' there the file IS the credential and an empty one is no login.
'
' The bridge is a TCP relay the agent reaches as host.docker.internal — which
' only the docker backend's bridge network resolves to the host, and only on the
' open+forward tier (every other tier parks the agent behind a sidecar with no
' route out but the proxy). This USED to exclude sbx too — see the correction
' at the top of this file; the two
' boxes exclude each other and are re-gated on every toggle.
'
' The gate is read from the FORM rather than from the row in hand: the sandbox
' box lives in the execution group and the Chrome box in the interface one, so
' the two are no longer neighbours in one slice. And from the row's OPTIONS
' rather than from On — the loop that ticks the compulsory box is what sets On,
' so asking On answers from the previous pass and leaves the host browser drawn
' live for one whole paint, long enough to accept.
'
' ── THE PREFLIGHT ASKS ABOUT THE AGENT'S ENVIRONMENT, NOT proveo'S ─────────
' Those differ, and the difference was a live bug. Two ways of being
' authenticated compete and do not merge, so AuthSuppressor drops every auth var
' of a provider whose login is already on disk — and the gate, reading the raw
' host environment, greyed the box over a CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN proveo was
' itself about to suppress. The run got the login and Chrome would have worked;
' the picker said it could not. Both halves come from AuthSuppressor rather than
' from a second rule that happens to agree, because the two disagreeing is what
' produced the bug.
'
' Reading the credential rule as "env-var sessions are refused" was the other
' half of the same mistake: it greyed the box for a correctly-scoped token that
' would have connected, and for an ANTHROPIC_API_KEY set beside a login the key
' does not displace. Offering the box would sell a bridge to a client that has
' already decided not to use it; withholding it hides one that works.
'
' DOCKER BACKEND ONLY. On sbx every outbound TCP is proxied and the VM "cannot
' access your host network directly" (Docker Sandboxes security docs), so the
' run never sets the variable there and the in-container half is a no-op.
'
' The launch flag is the ONLY thing that turns Chrome integration on for the
' session. `claudeInChromeDefaultEnabled` is deliberately NOT written to the
' operator's ~/.claude.json: it persists in the proveo home, and a later run
' without the add-on would then load browser tools that cannot connect, on
' every turn. The one-time onboarding dialog IS marked seen — it blocks on a
' keypress no automation answers, and it carries no posture.
'
' Before the browser integration is wired at all — BEFORE `--chrome` and before
' CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_CFC, so neither overrides it — Claude Code asks whether the
' session's OAuth scopes contain any of BrowserScopes, and logs
'
'   [Claude in Chrome] Disabled: OAuth token has no scope accepted by
'   /api/oauth/validate (needs user:profile, user:office, or user:ccr_inference;
'   env-var and setup-token sessions default to user:inference only)
'
' The endpoint is NAMED in the message but never called: the scopes are
' SYNTHESISED on the client from where the credential arrived. That is the whole
' rule, and it is why "an env-var session gets no Chrome" was too broad a
' reading — Anthropic's own cloud launcher passes CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN and
' gets Chrome, because it sets CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_SCOPES beside it.
'
' The env-token fallback is inference-only, which accepts nothing. `claude
' setup-token` mints exactly that shape, which is where the "setup-token
' sessions cannot use Chrome" folklore comes from — true, but as a consequence
' rather than as a rule.
'
' A persisted /login in the home this run mounts passes because its real scopes
' include user:profile; proveo cannot read them (macOS moves the token to the
' Keychain and blanks the file) and does not need to. A shape ScopeGate cannot
' classify returns "": the gate exists to spare the operator a bridge Claude
' Code has already decided not to use, not to invent refusals.

node "Operator's machine" as HOSTM <<host>> {
  component "Chrome + Claude in Chrome extension\n(the operator's logins, the extension's site permissions)" as CHROME <<cloud>>
  component "native messaging host\n""claude --chrome-native-host""" as NMH <<app>>
  database "LISTENS\n""/tmp/claude-mcp-browser-bridge-<user>/<pid>.sock""" as HSOCK <<db>>
  component "proveo run — ""internal/chromebridge""\nTCP relay, per-run token" as RELAY <<app>>
  component "picker add-on\n""claude-in-chrome"" — off by default" as PICK <<app>>
}

node "Agent container (docker backend)" as CONT <<host>> {
  component "chrome-bridge.js\n""proveo_chrome_bridge"" starts it before ""exec claude""" as CRELAY <<app>>
  database "LISTENS, same rule for <user>\n""/tmp/claude-mcp-browser-bridge-<user>/<pid>.sock""\n0700 dir · 0600 socket" as CSOCK <<db>>
  component "Claude Code ""--chrome""\nclaude-in-chrome MCP server (CONNECTS)" as CC <<app>>
}

component "sandbox VM (sbx)\nno route to the host" as SBX <<error>>
component "session with no accepted scope\n'Disabled: OAuth token has no scope…'" as SCOPE <<error>>
component "connection without the token\nclosed before a byte flows" as BAD <<error>>

CHROME ARROW_MAIN NMH : spawns, native messaging (stdio)
NMH    ARROW_MAIN HSOCK : creates, listens
CC     ARROW_MAIN CSOCK : newest ""*.sock"" in its dir
CSOCK  ARROW_MAIN CRELAY
CRELAY ARROW_MAIN RELAY : ""PROVEO_CHROME_BRIDGE=host.docker.internal:<port>""\nfirst line ""PROVEO-CHROME-BRIDGE <token>""
RELAY  ARROW_MAIN HSOCK : dials the newest socket,\npipes both ways
PICK   ARROW_MAIN RELAY : ticked → started for the run,\nenv + ""HostBridge"" on the egress plan
PICK   ARROW_ERROR SBX : greyed while docker (sandbox) is ticked —\nand on an sbx harness that box is now COMPULSORY,\nso ""PROVEO_SBX=0"" is the only way to reach this add-on
PICK   ARROW_ERROR SCOPE : greyed when the session's scopes\nname none of the three (""chromebridge.ScopeGate"")
RELAY  ARROW_ERROR BAD

note right of NMH
  **Who listens, who connects.** The native
  host is the server; Claude Code's MCP
  server is the client and picks the newest
  socket. Both halves of the bridge keep
  that shape, so neither Claude Code changes.
end note

note bottom of RELAY
  **TCP, because virtiofs drops sockets.** A
  bind mount cannot carry a Unix socket into
  Docker Desktop or OrbStack. The listener is
  loopback on macOS; the token is what guards
  Linux's bridge-facing address.
end note

note bottom of CRELAY
  **Same username rule as Claude Code.**
  ""os.userInfo().username"", else ""$USER"",
  ""$USERNAME"", ""default"". An arbitrary run-as
  uid lands on the fallback; guessing from
  the shell would miss the directory.
end note

note bottom of CC
  **--chrome is the only switch.**
  ""claudeInChromeDefaultEnabled"" is never
  persisted: the home outlives the run, and a
  later run would load browser tools that
  cannot connect. Onboarding IS marked seen.
end note

note right of SBX
  **Docker backend only.** Every outbound TCP
  of a sandbox is proxied on the host and the
  VM "cannot access your host network
  directly" (Docker Sandboxes security docs).
  Fallback prints why, never silently.
end note

note right of SCOPE
  **A rule about scopes, not variables.** The
  gate wants one of ""user:profile"",
  ""user:office"" or ""user:ccr_inference"", and it
  runs BEFORE ""--chrome"" and
  ""CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_CFC"" — neither overrides
  it. But ""/api/oauth/validate"" is only NAMED
  in the message: the scopes are synthesised
  on the client from where the credential came.

  ""CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN"" → whatever
  ""CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_SCOPES"" says, else the
  literal ""user:inference"" — which is what
  ""claude setup-token"" mints. File-descriptor
  delivery → a default carrying
  ""user:ccr_inference"". A ""/login"" store → its
  real scopes. Two of the three paths pass;
  Anthropic's own cloud launcher sets both env
  vars and gets Chrome.

  ""CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN"" SHADOWS the store.
  ""ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"" does not.

  **The gate asks what the AGENT will see, not
  what proveo was launched in.** Two ways of being
  authenticated compete and do not merge, so
  ""AuthSuppressor"" drops every auth var of a
  provider whose login is already on disk. Reading
  the raw host environment instead greyed the box
  over a token proveo was itself about to suppress:
  the run got the login and the bridge would have
  worked, while the picker said it could not. Both
  halves come from ""AuthSuppressor"" now, rather
  than from a second rule that happens to agree.
end note

note as SbxViewport #F4F9FB
  **The sandbox backend takes the other route.** This bridge points OUTWARD: the
  agent reaches the operator's own Chrome through a relay on the host, named as
  ""host.docker.internal"" — which only the docker backend's bridge network
  resolves, and which a sandbox VM cannot name at all. sbx publishes ports the
  other way (host → sandbox), so it can never serve this direction.

  What it can serve is the mirror image: the agent's OWN Chromium, published to
  host loopback so the operator attaches DevTools or Playwright to it. That is
  the browser add-on's viewport — _spec/internal/sbx/browser-viewport.puml.
end note

@enduml
